{"name":"Sequoia Redwood","hex":"#763f3d","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sequoia","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Sequoiadendron giganteum, a coniferous evergreen tree formerly in the genus Sequoia, now placed in Sequoiadendron.","Sequoia sempervirens, a coniferous evergreen tree, the only living species of the genus Sequoia."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sequoia"},{"term":"redwood","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A tree of the species Sequoia sempervirens.","Any of the evergreen conifers belonging to the genus Sequoia in the wide sense."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/redwood"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sequoia (genus)","blocks":[{"text":"Sequoia is a genus of redwood coniferous trees in the subfamily Sequoioideae of the family Cupressaceae. The only extant species of the genus is Sequoia sempervirens in the Northern California coastal forests ecoregion of Northern California and Southwestern Oregon in the United States. The two other genera in the subfamily Sequoioideae, Sequoiadendron and Metasequoia, are closely related to Sequoia. It includes the tallest trees, as well as the heaviest, in the world."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Sequoia_sempervirens1.jpg/330px-Sequoia_sempervirens1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sequoia_(genus)","wikidata":"Q1975652"},{"title":"Sequoioideae","blocks":[{"text":"Sequoioideae, commonly referred to as redwoods, is a subfamily of coniferous trees within the family Cupressaceae that range in the northern hemisphere. It includes the largest and tallest trees in the world. The trees in the subfamily are amongst the most distinctive trees in the world and are common ornamental trees. The subfamily reached its peak of diversity during the early Cenozoic. They are fast-growing trees that live in temperate climates such as Mediterranean and humid subtropical climates. Three living species are known, the coast redwood, the giant redwood and the dawn redwood. While the coast redwood and the giant redwood are found in the western United States, the dawn redwood is known from China."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Sequoioideae_extant_members.png/330px-Sequoioideae_extant_members.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sequoioideae","wikidata":"Q149335"},{"title":"Shades of red","blocks":[{"text":"Varieties of the color red may differ in hue, chroma, lightness, or in two or three of these qualities. Variations in value are also called tints and shades, a tint being a red or other hue mixed with white, a shade being mixed with black. A large selection of these various colors is shown below."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Shades_of_Red.svg/330px-Shades_of_Red.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_red","wikidata":"Q7460345"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Icicle_Tree_-_Armstrong_Redwoods_State_Reserve.jpeg/500px-Icicle_Tree_-_Armstrong_Redwoods_State_Reserve.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Icicle_Tree_-_Armstrong_Redwoods_State_Reserve.jpeg/1280px-Icicle_Tree_-_Armstrong_Redwoods_State_Reserve.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Icicle_Tree_-_Armstrong_Redwoods_State_Reserve.jpeg/500px-Icicle_Tree_-_Armstrong_Redwoods_State_Reserve.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"\"Icicle Tree\" showing burling of the trunk","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIcicle_Tree_-_Armstrong_Redwoods_State_Reserve.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Redwood_tree_in_Oakland_California_%28person_for_comparison%29IMG_4881.jpg/500px-Redwood_tree_in_Oakland_California_%28person_for_comparison%29IMG_4881.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Redwood_tree_in_Oakland_California_%28person_for_comparison%29IMG_4881.jpg/500px-Redwood_tree_in_Oakland_California_%28person_for_comparison%29IMG_4881.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Young but already tall redwood trees (Sequoia sempervirens) in Oakland, California","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARedwood_tree_in_Oakland_California_(person_for_comparison)IMG_4881.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/RGB_illumination.jpg/330px-RGB_illumination.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/RGB_illumination.jpg/330px-RGB_illumination.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARGB_illumination.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Twenty_hues_of_the_Munsell_color_system_at_maximum_chroma.png/500px-Twenty_hues_of_the_Munsell_color_system_at_maximum_chroma.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Twenty_hues_of_the_Munsell_color_system_at_maximum_chroma.png/1280px-Twenty_hues_of_the_Munsell_color_system_at_maximum_chroma.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Twenty_hues_of_the_Munsell_color_system_at_maximum_chroma.png/500px-Twenty_hues_of_the_Munsell_color_system_at_maximum_ch